# {{.Name}}

This starter serves an HTML welcome page and application health probes, with
explicit startup and graceful-shutdown wiring. Follow the dependency setup
below to run it locally.

This source belongs to your application. Change it directly; no generator
runtime, application generator state, or Arkoren configuration file is required.

## Install dependencies

Use Go 1.27 or newer. Scaffolding does not download dependencies.
From this application's directory, run:

```sh
go get arkoren.dev/core arkoren.dev/driver/mcp
go mod tidy
make run
```

These commands download dependencies. Commit `go.mod` and `go.sum`.

Open the `url` from the `http server started` log in a browser to see the welcome
page (by default, `http://127.0.0.1:8080/`).
Stop the service with Ctrl+C before building or running
another copy on the same port.

## Development commands

The generated `Makefile` belongs to your application; edit it as your workflow
grows. It requires Make and standard Unix tools, not the Arkoren CLI. Run `make`
for the command list. Without Make, you can still use `go run ./cmd/server`.

Use `make build` to write `bin/server`, `make fmt` to format Go source, and
`make check` to verify formatting, run static analysis, and test without changing
source files. `make vet` and `make test` run those checks separately.
`make test-race` enables CGO for that command and requires a C compiler.
Tests retain Go's normal caching. Dependency installation remains explicit;
these targets do not run `go get` or `go mod tidy` for you.

Override the Go executable with `make test GO=/path/to/go`. Formatting uses
`gofmt` from your `PATH`.

## Container image

The `Dockerfile` uses BuildKit source mounts and persistent module/compiler caches,
cross-compiles a stripped static binary, and runs it as a non-root user in a
minimal distroless image with CA certificates and timezone data. No shell or Go
toolchain ships in the runtime image. CGO-dependent drivers need a different
build and runtime image.

Build and run the image:

```sh
make docker-build
make docker-run
```

Open `http://127.0.0.1:8080/` to check the welcome page. Stop the container with
Ctrl+C; `--rm` removes it after it exits. Docker is required for these targets.
`docker-run` uses an existing image, sets `HTTP_ADDR=:8080` inside the container,
and disables debug diagnostics. The published host port binds only to loopback.
Override the image with `IMAGE=my-service:dev` on both commands, or change the
host port with `make docker-run PORT=8081`. BuildKit caches
downloads and compiled packages across source changes. Use Docker Buildx with
`--platform` to build for another Linux architecture. Pin base images by digest
when you need reproducible production builds.

`.gitignore` excludes local environment files, workspace settings, and build/test
output, but keeps `.env.example` and `go.sum`. `.dockerignore` also excludes local
environment files and Git/editor metadata from the build context; pass runtime
configuration explicitly rather than baking secrets into the image.

## HTTP and lifecycle

The executable imports `godotenv/autoload`, which loads `.env` from the working
directory during initialization. Existing environment variables take precedence;
loading errors are ignored. The generated `.env` selects
`HTTP_ADDR=127.0.0.1:8080` and `APP_DEBUG=true` for local development.
`bootstrap.Server(handler)` reads `HTTP_ADDR` inside its composition callback. An empty
address uses the server default `:8080` (all interfaces).

After successful startup and runner launch, the starter logs `http server started`
with an `address` field after `app started`. It reports the bound socket address,
including the assigned port if `HTTP_ADDR` selects port `0`, without guessing a
browser URL or public domain. `Server` uses `compose.MustDefer` to register the
subscription and own its cleanup, with the log callback inline. Startup failures produce no successful
HTTP startup log; readiness recovery does not repeat it.

In debug mode, `http://127.0.0.1:8080/mcp` exposes read-only application,
route, and telemetry diagnostics. The `http.routes` tool lists routes, handler
names, and source paths; it does not execute HTTP routes. `MCP(routes)` returns a factory that
constructs and mounts the endpoint. `bootstrap.Compose` selects it through
`compose.UseDebug(MCP(routes))`. It shares the main listener and inherited middleware. No authentication
is required: anyone who can reach that listener can read logs and telemetry
attributes as well as route diagnostics. Keep debug
off in production and bind development listeners to loopback.
From this directory, run `arkoren http:routes` to inspect the running service;
the CLI reads `ARKOREN_MCP_URL` from `.env`. If you change `HTTP_ADDR`, update
that URL too. No token is needed; clear any inherited `ARKOREN_MCP_TOKEN`.
Set `APP_DEBUG=false` in production to omit the MCP endpoint.

To inspect local requests, send a request to `/`, then run:

```sh
arkoren app:traces
arkoren app:logs
arkoren app:metrics
```

Debug capture retains up to 2000 finished spans, 5000 logs, 1000 metric series
per instrument, and 500 application event records in memory. It exports nothing
to an external backend and loses history when the process exits. The router's
tracing middleware adds a `traceresponse` header and ignores incoming trace
context. With debug disabled and no configured telemetry driver, it is a no-op.
Logs made with a request context carry that request's trace and span IDs.

The CLI creates `.env` with private access: mode 0600 on POSIX, or a protected
current-user-only DACL on Windows, before writing its contents. Keep it out of
version control; commit `.env.example`, which has debug disabled. Neither file needs
secrets for this starter. Generate independent keys with `arkoren key generate`
only when your application needs encryption or signing.

- `GET /` serves `app.WelcomeHandler()`, a public HTML welcome page. Replace
  this route as your application grows; it does not expose operator information.
- `GET /livez`, `/readyz`, and `/startupz` expose application health probes.
- `SIGINT` and `SIGTERM` cancel the context passed to `app.Run`, which
  owns startup and graceful shutdown.

## Browser security defaults

`bootstrap.Router` registers security headers, telemetry, panic recovery, and
same-origin CSRF protection before routes, in that order. A handler panic before
response commitment becomes a generic HTTP 500 problem without exposing the panic
value. Recovery does not replace an already committed response, and
`http.ErrAbortHandler` still aborts the connection. Responses prevent MIME sniffing and
framing, limit referrer disclosure, and disable the legacy XSS auditor. The
welcome page retains its stricter CSP. HSTS is omitted by default: enable it
in the application or ingress once your HTTPS deployment is established.

CSRF protection rejects untrusted cross-origin browser writes without requiring
domain settings. Keep GET, HEAD, and OPTIONS free of state changes. Requests
without Origin or Sec-Fetch-Site headers are allowed; this is not authentication.
Configure cross-origin frontends and provider callbacks deliberately, using
narrow route scopes rather than disabling protection globally. No extra trusted
origins or CORS permissions are configured. These defaults do not configure
authentication, accepted hosts, proxy trust, cookies, or rate limits, and do not
make the unauthenticated debug endpoint safe to expose publicly.

## Application execution

The executable's `main.go` constructs `app` with
`application.New(bootstrap.Application())` and service name `{{.Name}}`, then calls
`app.Run(ctx, bootstrap.Compose)` with a signal context. `bootstrap.Compose`
constructs dependencies before startup; `app.Run` returns composition, startup,
serving, or shutdown errors.
The application reports lifecycle failures before telemetry finalization; failures
before telemetry starts or during its finalization reach stderr only. The run
helper returns `app.Run` directly, and `main` maps failure to exit status 1;
clean shutdown returns 0. Its deferred signal cleanup
finishes before `main` calls `os.Exit`.

To customize logging, edit `Logger()` in `internal/bootstrap/logging.go`.
Logs go to stderr: terminals use Tint with source locations; redirected output
uses JSON. Set a nonempty `NO_COLOR` to disable terminal colors. Both formats
honor runtime log-level changes through `app.SetLogLevel`.

Bootstrap factories capture explicit dependencies. Their callbacks construct
resources only when composition invokes them, then return typed handles.

The composition root is `internal/bootstrap`: `bootstrap.go` composes components
before startup:

```go
routes := compose.Use(Router())
compose.UseDebug(MCP(routes))
compose.Use(Server(routes))
```

`Router` returns `application.Factory[*router.Router]`, so `Use` provides the
shared router directly to the dependent factories. `MCP` returns the mounted
`*mcpserver.Server`; `UseDebug` skips construction when debug is disabled. The
root ignores that optional result. `Server` returns `*server.Server` and owns
its `Cleanup`, `Startup`, and `Run` registrations and startup-log subscription.
The root ignores its result; `Use` itself does not
start the server. Obtaining any factory constructs nothing. `Router` gets
probes and the welcome handler from `compose.App()`. `app.go` supplies
`Application()`, `logging.go` supplies the `Logger()` handler factory,
`router.go` owns routes and middleware, `server.go` owns listener policy and
lifecycle, and `mcp.go` owns debug diagnostics. No `internal/config` package is
generated. The starter contains no tests; add them as behavior grows.

`.editorconfig` sets UTF-8, LF line endings, final newlines, tabs for Go and
`go.mod` and Makefile recipes, and two-space indentation elsewhere. Markdown keeps trailing spaces
for hard line breaks. Use an EditorConfig-compatible editor; `gofmt` remains
the source of truth for Go formatting.
